; field-reset-run.fk — start the shared field over.
;
; Every kernel on this host interns into one field (/fg-field-<letter>); the
; field persists across processes, as a host memory should. When it should be
; emptied -- a layout change, or the 2^26-cell ceiling near -- run this with no
; other kernel alive: it unlinks every field segment, and the next kernel to
; start opens a fresh one. With another kernel alive it refuses and names them.

(do
    (let others (kernel_live_pids))
    (let alive (len others))
    (if (gt alive 1)
        (print (list "field-reset refused: kernels alive" others))
        (print (list "field-reset" (field_reset) "the next kernel opens a fresh field"))))
